WEBVTT

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:16.268
Here is a question you have probably never asked. You drive past a wind farm, the blades turn, and somewhere behind them, electricity comes out. How?

00:00:16.453 --> 00:00:28.296
Nothing in the chapters so far explains it. Chapter 30 does, and the whole chapter is one sentence: a magnetic flux that changes, makes an emf.

00:00:29.126 --> 00:00:42.701
Section 30.1. Think of a bicycle dynamo. Pedal, and the lamp lights. Coast to a stop, and it dies. Nothing is plugged in; the motion itself makes the current.

00:00:42.886 --> 00:00:55.666
Faraday found the same thing with a magnet and a loop of wire. Push the magnet in: the needle swings. Pull it out: it swings the other way. Hold it still, even inside the loop: nothing.

00:00:56.026 --> 00:01:05.160
So it is not the magnet, and it is not the field. What the loop feels is the magnetic flux through it, and only its change.

00:01:05.345 --> 00:01:15.065
Picture the flux as the number of field lines caught in the loop. You can change it three ways: a stronger field, a bigger loop, or a tilt.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:23.051
That is the whole of Faraday's law. The emf is the rate at which the flux changes, times the number of turns.

00:01:23.481 --> 00:01:30.985
Pause and predict. Push the magnet in slowly, then push it in fast. Which gives the bigger emf?

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:44.183
Fast. Same change of flux, but in less time, so a bigger rate. The dynamo says the same: pedal faster, brighter lamp.

00:01:44.543 --> 00:01:56.527
The pitfall: a strong field is not a large emf. A loop parked inside an MRI magnet, the strongest field you will ever meet, gives nothing at all, because nothing is changing.

00:01:56.747 --> 00:02:00.503
A flux that changes. Nothing else makes an emf.

00:02:01.403 --> 00:02:14.868
Section 30.2. Walk a metal trolley through the security gate at the airport. The faster you push it, the more the gate pushes back. Where does that resistance come from?

00:02:15.053 --> 00:02:27.080
Keep the field steady, and move the conductor instead. The free electrons in the metal are now moving charges in a magnetic field, so the field pushes them to one end of the rod.

00:02:27.265 --> 00:02:39.673
That piling up of charge is a voltage. For a rod of length ell, moving at speed v across a field B, it is simply B, ell, v. The motional emf.

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:49.396
Put the rod on two rails with a resistor, and a current flows round the loop. Faraday agrees: the loop is getting bigger, so its flux is growing.

00:02:49.581 --> 00:02:57.673
Pause and predict. You pull the bar at a steady speed, and the resistor gets warm. Where does that energy come from?

00:03:00.658 --> 00:03:13.754
From your hand. The current in the bar sits in the field, so the field pushes back on the bar, like the gate on the trolley. You push against that force, and every joule of heat in the resistor came from you.

00:03:14.114 --> 00:03:24.613
The pitfall: double the speed and you double the emf and the current. But the force on the bar doubles too, so the power you supply goes up four times, not two.

00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:29.229
B ell v, and the energy comes from whoever is pushing.

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:43.440
Section 30.3. Drop a magnet down a copper pipe and it falls in slow motion, as if the pipe were full of honey. Nothing touches it. What is holding it back?

00:03:43.625 --> 00:03:58.294
The magnet's flux through each ring of copper is changing, so currents flow, and those currents make a field of their own. Lenz's law says which way: the induced field always opposes the change.

00:03:58.479 --> 00:04:11.401
Push a north pole toward a loop and the loop answers with a north pole of its own, and pushes back. Pull it away and the loop flips to a south pole, and holds on. Either way, the loop fights the motion.

00:04:11.761 --> 00:04:20.097
Pause and predict. Suppose the loop helped instead of fighting. Give a bar on rails one small push. What happens next?

00:04:23.082 --> 00:04:32.591
It would run away. More speed, more flux change, more current, more push, more speed, for ever, with no one paying. Energy from nothing.

00:04:32.776 --> 00:04:52.393
That cannot happen, so the sign has to be the other one. Lenz's law is simply energy conservation, in disguise. And it is why the magnet in the pipe falls slowly, and why a hybrid car slows down when you lift your foot: the motor turns into a generator, and the generator fights the wheels.

00:04:52.753 --> 00:05:06.215
The pitfall: Lenz's law does not say the induced field opposes the magnet's field. It opposes the change. When the flux is falling, the induced field points the same way as the magnet's, trying to keep it up.

00:05:06.435 --> 00:05:08.808
Oppose the change, not the flux.

00:05:09.708 --> 00:05:25.014
Section 30.4. Put your phone on a wireless charger. No plug, no metal contact, nothing moves. Yet energy crosses the gap. Something in that gap is pushing charges along.

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:38.765
Here is the hidden point of the whole chapter. In a loop sitting in a changing field, the charges are at rest, so no magnetic force can move them. What pushes them is an electric field.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:52.076
A changing magnetic field creates an electric field, in empty space, rings of it, whether or not there is a wire. Take the wire away and the field is still there. The loop was only ever a detector.

00:05:52.436 --> 00:06:05.511
Written down, that is Faraday's law in its general form: go once round any closed path, add up the electric field along it, and you get minus the rate of change of the flux inside.

00:06:05.941 --> 00:06:14.912
Pause and predict. For the electric field of a charge, that trip round a closed path always adds up to zero. What about this new field?

00:06:17.897 --> 00:06:31.235
Not zero. Its lines have no beginning and no end; they close on themselves. So there is no voltage you can attach to it, no potential, no high point and low point. A field with no hills.

00:06:31.595 --> 00:06:43.061
The pitfall: never write V equals minus the integral of E for an induced field. Potential belongs to charges at rest. This field belongs to a changing B.

00:06:43.281 --> 00:06:45.893
The loop was only ever a detector.

00:06:46.793 --> 00:07:00.248
Section 30.5. The wind-up torch: crank the handle and the light comes on. Inside, there is a coil, a magnet, and nothing else. Now we can say exactly what is happening.

00:07:00.433 --> 00:07:15.984
Turning a coil in a field is the third knob, the tilt, turned continuously. The flux goes up and down like a wave, so the emf is a wave too, and it is largest when the coil is edge-on, where the flux is changing fastest.

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:29.813
That wave is the AC generator, the thing behind the wind turbine's blades. Its peak is N, B, A, omega: turns, field, area, speed. Four knobs, and that is the whole list.

00:07:29.998 --> 00:07:39.358
Swap the slip rings for a split ring, and the connections flip every half turn: the output never goes negative. That is the DC generator.

00:07:39.718 --> 00:07:48.639
Pause and predict. Which of these does not raise the emf: thicker wire, faster spin, a stronger magnet, more turns?

00:07:51.624 --> 00:07:59.548
Thicker wire. It can carry more current, but the emf has four knobs, and resistance is not one of them.

00:07:59.908 --> 00:08:16.390
Now run it backwards. Feed the generator a current, and it turns: a motor. But a turning coil in a field is also a generator, so the motor makes its own back emf, which fights the supply. The faster it spins, the less current it draws.

00:08:16.575 --> 00:08:25.637
The pitfall: a jammed drill. Stall the motor, and the back emf vanishes, so it draws its biggest current of all, and that is why it gets hot.

00:08:25.857 --> 00:08:29.525
A generator run backwards is a motor, and it fights you.

00:08:30.425 --> 00:08:32.018
Five things to keep.

00:08:32.203 --> 00:08:39.061
One: only a changing flux makes an emf. Field, area, or tilt.

00:08:39.246 --> 00:08:46.384
Two: move a conductor through a field and you get B ell v, paid for by whoever pushes.

00:08:46.569 --> 00:08:51.945
Three: the induced current fights the change, because energy is conserved.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:57.687
Four: a changing B makes an electric field in space, with or without a wire.

00:08:57.872 --> 00:09:04.758
Five: spin a coil and you have a generator; feed it, and it is a motor that fights back.

00:09:05.188 --> 00:09:10.410
The mind map and the self-check for this chapter are on the course page. See you in chapter 31.
